[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Decomposition Strategies & Strangler Fig Pattern in Microservices"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Migrating a monolithic application to a microservices architecture is a complex undertaking. Attempting a complete rewrite all at once is notoriously risky and often leads to project failure. Instead, software architects rely on systematic decomposition strategies and incremental migration patterns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Decomposition Strategies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before applying migration patterns, you must decide how to slice the monolith. Effective decomposition ensures that resulting microservices are loosely coupled and highly cohesive."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decompose by Business Capability: Align microservices with the organization's business domains (e.g., Billing, Shipping, User Management)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decompose by Subdomain: Utilize Domain-Driven Design (DDD) to identify core, supporting, and generic subdomains."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decompose by Volatility: Isolate parts of the application that change frequently from those that remain stable."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Strangler Fig Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Named after the strangler fig vine that seeds in the branches of a fig tree and eventually grows around it to replace it, this pattern involves incrementally replacing functionality by creating a new system around the edges of the old one."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1: Intercept requests using a facade, API Gateway, or reverse proxy to route traffic between the monolith and new services."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2: Extract a specific piece of functionality (e.g., Notification Service) and build it as a microservice."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 3: Route traffic for that specific capability to the new microservice via the API Gateway."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 4: Repeat the process until the monolith is completely 'strangled' and decommissioned."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing an API Gateway Router"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a simple NGINX configuration acting as a router for the Strangler Fig pattern, directing specific endpoints to a new microservice while keeping the rest routed to the monolith."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"events {} 

http {
    upstream monolith {
        server legacy-app:8080;
    }

    upstream user_service {
        server user-microservice:5000;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;

        location /api/v1/users {
            proxy_pass http://user_service;
        }

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://monolith;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Migration Challenges"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A major hurdle during decomposition is database refactoring. Monoliths often share a single database where tables have tight foreign-key constraints. To safely migrate data, teams frequently employ the Database-per-Service pattern alongside change data capture (CDC) tools like Debezium to synchronize data between the old monolith and the new microservice."}]
